```

**(a)** Write the complete class `Perishable`, a subclass of `Item`. A `Perishable` item also has a number of days left before it spoils.

- The constructor `Perishable(String name, double price, int daysLeft)` sets the item's name, regular price and days left.
- The method `getPrice` is overridden: when 2 or fewer days are left, the price is half the regular price; otherwise it is the regular price.
- The method `toString` is overridden to return the text produced by the `Item` version followed by a space and the number of days left in parentheses, for example `"Milk: 2.0 (2 days)"` for an item named Milk with a regular price of 4.0 and 2 days left.

**(b)** Write the method `cheapest`, which returns the `Item` in the list with the lowest value of `getPrice()`. If more than one item has the lowest price, return the one that appears first. Precondition: the list contains at least one item.

```java
public static Item cheapest(ArrayList<Item> items)
```

sampleResponse:
```java
public class Perishable extends Item {
    private int daysLeft;

    public Perishable(String name, double price, int daysLeft) {
        super(name, price);
        this.daysLeft = daysLeft;
    }

    public double getPrice() {
        if (daysLeft <= 2) {
            return super.getPrice() / 2;
        }
        return super.getPrice();
    }

    public String toString() {
        return super.toString() + " (" + daysLeft + " days)";
    }
}

public static Item cheapest(ArrayList<Item> items) {
    Item best = items.get(0);
    for (Item it : items) {
        if (it.getPrice() < best.getPrice()) {
            best = it;
        }
    }
    return best;
}
```
`Item.toString()` calls `getPrice()` on the actual object, so a discounted `Perishable` prints its discounted price. The strict `<` keeps the first item when prices tie.
